# Can dots make light? Seurat's A Sunday on La Grande Jatte
tags: ["art history","pointillism","seurat","color theory","optical mixing","renaissance painting"]
teaches: ["art-history/pointillism-technique","concepts/optical-color-mixing","concepts/color-relationships","methods/scientific-painting"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What happens to the dots when you step back? Why did Seurat think this would make colours brighter?"
next: {"title":"Can light be a feeling? Van Gogh's The Starry Night","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/how-painters-caught-light/10-can-light-be-a-feeling-van-gogh-s-the.cast"}

## La Grande Jatte: can dots make light?
sources:
    - id: wiki
      title: A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: 1884–86, 207.6 × 308 cm, Art Institute of Chicago; dots mixed in the eye, after Chevreul and Rood, believed to make colours more brilliant
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Sunday_Afternoon_on_the_Island_of_La_Grande_Jatte
    - id: chevreul
      title: Michel Eugène Chevreul
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Director of the Gobelins dye works from 1824; a black that looked weak beside blues and purples led him to simultaneous contrast
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michel_Eug%C3%A8ne_Chevreul
    - id: divisionism
      title: Divisionism
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Optical mixture averages the luminosities of neighbouring colours; contrast gives a mild shimmer rather than extra brightness
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisionism

    card title "A Sunday on La Grande Jatte"

A: This is Seurat's A Sunday on La Grande Jatte, three metres of summer afternoon on an island in the Seine —
    image art cites ["wiki"] credit "Seurat, 1884–86. Art Institute of Chicago. Public domain." x 500 y 360 width 890 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Georges_Seurat_-_A_Sunday_on_La_Grande_Jatte_--_1884_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Georges_Seurat_-_A_Sunday_on_La_Grande_Jatte_--_1884_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.couple [0.72,0.13,0.23,0.77] regions.crowd [0.05,0.25,0.9,0.7] regions.girl [0.622,0.426,0.068,0.2] regions.girl_head [0.63,0.435,0.047,0.07] regions.jacket [0.785,0.24,0.06,0.22] regions.lawn [0.25,0.45,0.5,0.45] regions.river [0,0.15,0.3,0.42] regions.shadow [0.52,0.66,0.12,0.13] regions.skirt [0.8,0.45,0.14,0.42] regions.sunlit [0.37,0.47,0.06,0.12] regions.sunlit2 [0.47,0.58,0.08,0.07] regions.sunlit3 [0.02,0.6,0.09,0.1]

A: and almost all of it is dots.
    highlight art:lawn strength soft

B: Dots? From here I see grass, parasols, and a lot of people in their Sunday best.
    highlight art:crowd strength soft

A: Come close, and a face falls apart into specks of colour.
    image dots x 290 y 430 width 360 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Detail_Seurat_Grande_Jatte.jpg look screen reveal develop

B: That's a face? It looks like orange and blue confetti.
    point at.ref dots gesture glow

    ask "Where in the painting is this head? Tap it." spot girl_head on art voice a
        right: There: the girl bending over her little bunch of flowers.
        wrong: Look for a girl with her head bowed over a few flowers.

    erase dots

    camera on ["art:girl"] zoom 2.5

A: From a step back, your eye does the mixing: the specks melt into skin, hair and shade.
    highlight art:girl_head lift true

    camera reset true

    portrait seurat of "Georges Seurat" cameo true

A: Seurat was twenty-four when he began it, in 1884, and he worked on it for two years.
    camera center.ref art:crowd zoom 1.12 duration 5

    erase seurat

    camera reset true

B: Monet caught his sunrise in a morning. Why go dot by dot?
    highlight art:river strength soft lift true

A: He wanted light by rule, not by instinct, and took his rules from science —
    highlight art:crowd strength soft

A: one from a chemist, Chevreul, who ran the dye works at the Gobelins tapestry factory in Paris.
    portrait chevreul of "Michel Eugène Chevreul" cameo true

B: Let me guess: the weavers wanted brighter dyes.
    camera center.ref art:crowd zoom 1.12 duration 4

    erase chevreul

    camera on ["art:couple"]

A: They complained that a black looked weak.
    highlight art:jacket lift true

A: But the dye was fine: it only looke (@highlight art:skirt lift true@)d weak beside the blues and purples woven next to it.

B: So a colour depends on its neighbours.
    highlight art:jacket art:skirt

    camera reset true

A: Yes. And he'd read that mixing paint has a cost: stir two colours together, and the mixture comes out duller.
    highlight art:girl strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art:girl"] zoom 2.5

A: So Seurat set small touches of colour side by side and let them mix in the eye, which, he believed, would keep them bright, like light itself.
    highlight art:girl_head lift true

    camera reset true

    quiz "What did Seurat sow into the grass's shadow? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 500 buttons_at.y 560 voice a
        intro: So how does he darken a shadow?
        right: Blue: a cool colour that reads as shade, with no black at all.
        wrong: A shadow on a sunny day looks cool, not dirty.
        * Black dots
        * Brown dots
        * Only darker green
        + Blue dots

    camera on ["art:shadow"] zoom 3

A: Deep green, with blue dots sown through it: shade made of cool colour.
    highlight art:shadow lift true

    camera on ["art:sunlit"] zoom 3

A: And in the sunlit grass, yellow with orange dots: warm against cool, each making the other stronger.
    highlight art:sunlit lift true

    camera reset true

B: So the sunshine isn't painted on top. It's in every patch.
    highlight art:sunlit3 art:sunlit art:sunlit2 strength strong

A: The honest catch: the eye averages the dots, so they're never brighter than the paints themselves. What they give instead is a shimmer, like air on a hot day.
    highlight art:lawn lift true

B: Two years of science, and the reward is a shimmer.
    highlight art:sunlit2 strength soft lift true

A: A shimmer over a still crowd: the people stand like statues while the light hums around them.
    highlight art:couple strength strong rest grey lift true

B: So Monet grabbed the light as it went by, and Seurat… built it?
    highlight art:river strength soft lift true

A: Built it, dot by dot, and left the last step to your eyes.
    highlight art:girl_head lift true

    quiz "A mosaic maker wants a sunny wall to glow from across the square. Which follows Seurat?" voice a
        right: Warm and cool small tiles side by side: the eye mixes them from across the square, and the cool shade makes the warm glow.
        wrong: Seurat left the mixing to the viewer's eye, and let warm meet cool.
        * Tiles in pre-mixed colours
        * Black tiles for every shadow
        + Small tiles, warm and cool side by side
        * Small tiles of gold, grey in the shade

A: Next time a screen looks smooth, lean in: it's tiny dots too, and your eye is still finishing the job.
    draw dots

